st and  what I'm saying is food is no longer  entertainment for me I treated it for  many years as though it was  entertainment and now I can treat the  experience of the the meal with someone  else as entertainment or even the  experience of cooking the food as an  inert way of entertaining myself but I  think entertainment shouldn't be the  point here and I have a very short essay  from our book essential called  food is not entertainment and I'd like  to read that to you very quickly food is  not entertainment these four words help  me change my dietary mindset which  helped me change my eating habits which  helped me drop from 240 pounds to 160  pounds in two and a half years fat too  thin unhealthy too healthy flabby to fit  accordingly I no longer treat food as  entertainment rather food is meant to  nourish food is fuel plain and simple  that doesn't mean I don't enjoy food  though I enjoy it immensely I eat with  friends I share meals with loved ones I  drink coffee with strangers the  difference is I don't turn to food to  entertain me to comfort me or to quote  to get me through tough times I turn to  food to power my body to make me feel  great to keep me healthy these things  come first and then I'm free to enjoy  each meal so I want that I want to just  expand on that a little bit one I was  uneducated on food  yeah I thought French fries were an  acceptable vegetable because they were  potatoes and and no idea you know the  difference between they're basically  three types of food right you have you  have carbohydrates you have protein and  you have ketones and when I look at your  average healthy extremely healthy person  they've radically reduced the number of  carbohydrates they they take in every  single day so that starts with sugar and  it even goes as far as rice and starchy  vegetables like potatoes and things like  that but then of course there's the  there are very few if any health  benefits of eating any kind of processed  foods right unless you're in a situation  where you're starving or there's  malnourishment or something like that  but if you're in a situation like like I  was where I was just eating to pacify  myself I'd get that the moment of what  Joe Rogan calls mouth pleasure you get  that moment of mouth pleasure and and  the consequences are just dire and so I  wanted to also talk just briefly about  what misty and in Rochelle asked and  their questions about finding healthy  food finding a healthy diet that is also  sort of cost-effective or low cost was  the word that was used misty said she  was overspending and Rochelle want to  know about low-cost our budgeting for  for healthy food and let me tell you  eating unhealthy food is always going to  be of a much higher cost long term not  monetarily necessarily although probably  monetarily too when you get the hospital  bills from from a life of of poor health  I know my mother was 63 and she found  out she had stage 4 lung cancer that was  from a life of poor decisions  with respect to smoking now many people  in her generation she was born in 1945  many people of her generation smoked  their entire lives and a lot of them  developed lung cancer because of it and  a lot of other diseases that go along  with our diet whether it's heart disease  the number one killer everything from  that to gout or generally because of the  diet that we have and so you're going to  end up paying for healthy the the you're  gonna be paying for the food whether  it's the healthy food you're gonna pay a  little bit more for upfront or you're  gonna pay for it with your health or  your time or your life ultimately so  that's the first thing to think about  now let's get a little more practical  when I was first simplifying my life and  I was first really trying to get through  the the my diet and figure out okay how  do i how do i simplify this diet still  be able to afford it well i was about  putting a budget together and one of the  things i budgeted for was healthy food  and sometimes i had to say no to other  things in my life other material  purchases i had to find ways to reduce  other expenses in my life so that I  could make sure I could afford the  healthiest food and by the way buying  healthy food right now is gonna save you  a ton of money in the long run so just  think about that next time you're at the  the checkout line and you have some  packaged foods or whatever how could I  better spend this money on on something  healthy okay now it's time for our added  value portion of the show where we  recommend something that has added value  to our lives recently and since we're  talking about health we're gonna  recommend at least one podcast here  bulletproof radio a guy named Dave  Asprey who is I guess you could call him  a bio hacker he was significantly  overweight and allergic to mold and a  bunch of other stuff and he had a lot of  issues and he radically changed his life  over the course of several years he's  written a book I think it's called like  the the bulletproof diet or a  bulletproof cookbook but he has a great  podcast as well  it's called bulletproof radio where he  interviews a bunch of different doctors  nutritionists etc  and and gets their take their expertise  into the conversation and another  podcast I've really found a lot of value  in a lot of it's over my head because  she gets very much into the weeds with  with a lot of scientific nomenclature  and and what one might consider jargon  but I am able to tweeze out a lot of  nuggets from Rhonda Patrick's podcast  she's over at found my fitness is the  name of her podcast so you can check  that out I also recommended a bunch of  stuff throughout this episode whether it  was the nutribullet or some of the stuff  so check the show notes for some other  recommendations in there as well I hope  you find value in those show notes and  then I'm gonna move on to right here  right now this is where I get to stop  talking about myself and star